FAIL (blocking)

Preflight of `writing-collection` (Level 3). Units 1-4 encode TDD correctly (stub, tests, red, code, green). No change-detector steps and no misordered steps, so no in-phase plan edits were made. The plan is not acceptable as written because of finding 1. Findings 2-4 are fixable inside the same re-plan.

## Blocking

1. **TDD gap in unit 5 (blocking).** Unit 5 is labelled prose/policy with "No tests", but it contains Liquid that computes behavior: `pages/writing/tags.md` omits tags with fewer than 2 pieces, `pages/writing/categories.md` keeps a one-piece category, `pages/writing.md` and `_data/writing_menu.yaml` list `site.writing` by date, and the `pages/index.md` block renders under the posts menu. Units 3 and 4 already treat the same kind of Liquid (layouts) as executable and test it. Only the three seed files and the `_plugins/README.md` note are prose. The test plan lists no case for `/writing/`, `/writing/tags/`, `/writing/categories/`, or the home-page block. That leaves brief acceptance criteria 3 (indexes list only writing) and 4 (`/all-posts.html`, recent list, garden index omit seeds) with no test, and it leaves the singleton-tag and one-piece-category rules unproven on the index pages. Required change: split the index pages and menu data into an executable unit with test steps first, and keep the seeds as the prose unit.

## Fixable

2. **LLM category scopes leak into the posts namespace (verified).** `llm_sidecars.include_categories: true` makes the gem build one scope per category found in the census, at `jekyll-archives.permalinks.category` (`/categories/:name/`). I built a throwaway site with the plan's `post_init` hook, `include_paths: [writing]` and `include_categories: true`. It emitted `/categories/poetry/llms.txt` and `/categories/poetry/llms-full.txt`. So adding `writing` to `include_paths` puts writing into `/categories/`, which the plan's Boundary Changes say stays posts-only. If a post category ever shares a name with a folder, the two scopes share one path. Unit 4 also says to register a writing category scope, but no test covers it and the plan does not say how to stop the gem's own scope. The plan needs a decision (for example `include_categories: false` plus explicit builders for post categories and writing categories), a test that `/categories/poetry/llms.txt` is absent, and a test that the writing category scope exists.
3. **`exclude_paths` omits writing archive pages.** The existing config excludes `/tags/**/*`, `/categories/**/*` and `/garden/tags/**/*` so archive pages do not get `.md` sidecars. Unit 4 only adds `writing` to `include_paths`. `/writing/tags/**/*` and `/writing/categories/**/*` need the same exclusion, plus an assertion that no `.md` sidecar exists beside an archive page.
4. **REUSE licensing.** `REUSE.toml` gives creative writing under `blog/**` and `_garden/**` CC-BY-SA-4.0 by override. `_writing/**` would fall to the AGPL default. CI runs `reuse lint`, so the build will still pass, but the seeds get the wrong license. Add `_writing/**` to the CC-BY-SA-4.0 annotation. This is a config change with no test.

## Advisory

5. **Brief AC1 versus the planned URLs.** AC1 says `/writing/poetry/`, `/writing/songs/` and `/writing/stories/` each resolve to one seed piece. The plan puts the piece at `/writing/poetry/porch-light.html` and the category archive at `/writing/categories/poetry/`. Nothing is published at `/writing/poetry/`. The brief's use-case text says the URL is `/writing/<folder>/<slug>`, so AC1 is probably shorthand. Confirm that reading with the operator, or add an index at the folder path. No test asserts that the three seed URLs exist either.
6. **Fixture needs.** The writing fixture must copy `navigational_tags.rb` (used by `garden_archives.rb` and `20_llms_scope_builders.rb`) and `collection_dates.rb` (the layout's Updated line reads `last_modified`, which only that plugin sets). It must also copy the local `_includes/back_link.html` and stub the theme's `default` layout (missing layout is only a warning). The author-page case also needs `jekyll-auto-authors`, `jekyll-paginate-v2` (load order matters per the Gemfile), `_data/authors.yaml` and `email_obfuscator.rb` for the `{% email %}` tag. If that is too heavy, test `author-archive.html` through a hand-written page.
7. **Existing facts confirmed.** `Document#initialize` calls `categories_from_path("_writing")`, which yields no categories for files under the collection folder, then fires `post_init`. A later `merge_categories!` unions with the hook's value. `UrlDrop#categories` and `#title` give `/writing/poetry/porch-light.html`. The filename date and slug come from `populate_title`. The feed template has a single `site[page.collection]` assignment, which the planned substitution targets. Only tag archives are filtered for singletons in `garden_archives.rb`, so one-piece category archives are published as planned. `site.tags`, `site.categories`, `/all-posts.html` and `site.posts` are posts-only.
8. **Radical innovation (advisory).** Three plugins now hard-code collection names: `garden_in_feed.rb` (garden, plus writing), `20_llms_scope_builders.rb` (garden tags, plus writing) and the `garden_archives.rb` special case for `garden`. Replace them with config lists. Add `feed.concat_collections: [garden, writing]` read by the feed plugin. Make the scope builder loop over `jekyll-archives.collections` keys to register tag and category scopes. A fourth collection then costs configuration only, and the same loop is the natural place for the category-scope decision in finding 2. This costs a small refactor of two plugins and their tests now.
